# Read-only language services

Once a server understands the projected program, the editor can ask ordinary
programming questions inside a fragment: What does this name mean? What could be
inserted here? Which parameter comes next? Where is this symbol declared or used?

These questions are read-only in the architectural sense. Their answers may display
text, offer an insertion at the cursor, or navigate to a location, but the server is
not given authority to execute a command or apply a workspace-wide change. Even a
completion item is reduced to one carefully bounded replacement in the source
occurrence that initiated the request.

The supported question families are hover information, completion, signature help,
definition, type definition, implementation, and references. Each family is enabled
only when the initialized server advertises the corresponding capability.

## Every request keeps its occurrence

A request begins with the captured literate document and cursor. Projection finds a
literal mapping and chooses its first deterministic generated occurrence. The owner
synchronizes the relevant projected documents, sends the request at the generated
UTF-16 position, and retains the exact mapping object used to get there.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Source as Literate source
    participant Projection
    participant Server
    participant Guard as Reply validator

    Source->>Projection: Cursor in literal fragment text
    Projection->>Projection: Select one generated occurrence
    Projection->>Server: Request at generated UTF-16 position
    Server-->>Guard: Untrusted reply
    Guard->>Guard: Check shape, bounds, occurrence, freshness
    Guard-->>Source: Safe display, insertion, or location
```

The return path is intentionally narrower than the outward path. The server sees a
complete projected file, but a reply can affect only information justified by the
captured mapping and the feature's policy. The diagram omits session startup and
queueing, which Chapter 19 and Chapter 23 treat separately.

After every asynchronous boundary, Literate checks that the document revision,
project inputs, workspace, trust, configuration, and request cancellation still
match. A valid reply to an obsolete question is still obsolete and is discarded.

## Hover text is display data, not authority

A hover reply may contain plain text, Markdown, or a language-marked code block.
Literate bounds that content and constructs untrusted Markdown with embedded HTML
disabled. A link or markup sequence therefore does not acquire command authority
merely because a server returned it.

If the server supplies an explicit hover range, the complete range must map through
the original occurrence, remain on the cursor's source line, and contain the cursor.
An absent range uses the current source word. A malformed or unsafe explicit range
causes the hover to be omitted rather than widened to a nearby word.

This distinction preserves meaning. Hover contents can still be useful without an
invented source span, but once a server claims a precise span, presenting a different
one would misrepresent its answer.

## Completion is a controlled local insertion

A completion list can contain plain text or snippets. Literate accepts bounded
labels, documentation, details, sorting metadata, standard item kinds, and a narrow
set of list defaults. Unknown defaults are rejected when they could alter insertion
semantics.

The replacement range must satisfy all of these conditions:

- it belongs to the exact mapping used for the request;
- it lies on the cursor's source line;
- it contains the cursor;
- an insert-and-replace pair shares one start and has a contained insertion range;
- its coordinates fit both projected and source text in UTF-16.

Without an explicit edit, Literate replaces the current identifier-like source word,
not the entire expression before the cursor. After punctuation, or when no safe word
exists, the result becomes a zero-width insertion at the cursor.

Commands and auxiliary edits are not retained. This excludes a completion that
would insert one name locally while silently changing imports or another file. A
snippet still uses the editor's normal snippet interpreter, but its tab stops do not
grant access to additional source ranges.

Some servers resolve a selected completion later to add expensive information.
Literate keeps the already validated insertion fixed. Resolution may add only
bounded documentation and detail; it cannot replace the text, range, label, or
attach executable behavior. If the source or session is no longer fresh, resolution
adds nothing.

## Signature help validates labels independently

Signature help displays callable forms and their parameters. A parameter may name a
substring of the signature label or provide a pair of UTF-16 offsets into that
label. Literate checks that names occur and offset pairs are ordered and within the
label before constructing editor objects.

Active signature and parameter indexes are also bounded. An out-of-range selection
falls back to the first valid entry rather than indexing arbitrary data. Documentation
passes through the same untrusted Markdown boundary used by hovers and completions.

Server-advertised trigger and retrigger characters influence when the coordinator
asks for signature help, but they do not weaken reply validation. Chapter 23 explains
how those characters are learned without turning all Markdown typing into language
requests.

## Navigation has two kinds of destination

A navigation reply may point into a projected output or into an ordinary local file,
such as a library declaration.

A known projected output must map back to captured literate source. Wrapper text,
inserted indentation, fragment markers, and other unmapped generated ranges yield no
location. A valid ordinary local `file:` URI may remain a direct destination after
its structure and range are checked. The mapper does not open that file or perform
filesystem I/O to verify its contents.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Reply[Server location] --> URI{Safe absolute local file URI?}
    URI -->|No| Omit[Omit location]
    URI -->|Yes| Known{Known projected output?}
    Known -->|Yes| Mapped{Range intersects captured literal source?}
    Mapped -->|Yes| Source[Return literate source location]
    Mapped -->|No| Omit
    Known -->|No| Local[Return validated local-file location]
```

The arrows show validation decisions. The direct local-file path is not a mapping
fallback for known outputs: once a URI identifies a projected file, missing mapping
evidence causes omission.

Only absolute local file URIs without authorities, queries, fragments, encoded
control characters, or path ambiguities are accepted. Location links must provide a
valid enclosing target range; a preferred selection must itself be valid and remain
inside that target.

## Seams are handled without invented source

A navigation range can cross several generated segments. Literate selects the first
literal intersection in generated order and extends it only while generated and
source offsets remain contiguous. It does not create one source range that encloses
intervening prose, fragment declarations, or reference markers.

The only allowed source gap is the carriage-return unit omitted when a source line
ending is normalized. This special case preserves one logical newline without
pretending that unrelated text is contiguous.

Repeated locations are deduplicated, and reply traversal is bounded. A repeated
fragment or repeated template slot may send several generated locations back to the
same source interval; the editor presents that source location once. Distinct source
occurrences that happen to contain equal text remain distinct.

`navigation intersections` walks mapped segments monotonically and stops when
continuity cannot be proved.

```ts : <<navigation intersections>>=
function mapped(file: LanguageProjection, bounds: [number, number], state: Prepared, limit: number): vscode.Location[] {
    const result: vscode.Location[] = [], seen = new Set<string>();
    let pending: Intersection | undefined;
    const flush = (): void => {
        if (!pending) { return; }
        const value = location(pending), id = key(value);
        if (!seen.has(id)) { seen.add(id); result.push(value); }
    };
    for (const mapping of file.mappings) {
        const next = intersection(file, mapping, bounds[0], bounds[1], state);
        if (!next) { continue; }
        if (pending && connected(pending, next)) { pending.end = next.end; pending.to = next.to; continue; }
        flush();
        if (result.length >= limit) { return result; }
        pending = next;
    }
    flush();
    return result;
}
```

Read-only services can safely prefer omission because their purpose is guidance.
The user loses one answer rather than being sent to a fabricated location or given
an unsafe replacement. Diagnostics require broader, persistent ownership, so the
next chapter adds time, caching, and the editor's Problems collection to the mapping
problem.
